#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130203} :activity/id #uuid "1e370681-4235-581a-8e92-bfa4ed63d765" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050390 :topic/id #uuid "4543eb7d-4646-5b7f-9bb9-14d0a8526a45" :topic/math-academy-id 2060} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130455 :tutorial/content "When evaluating triple integrals, it's sometimes easier to change the variables from Cartesian coordinates to spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2\\right)^{3/2} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the region enclosed by the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/08/0809a74f0aca2b3042b7f8db6783f9d6a13bc84bfc75ddcbb4d3076dbf0a2efd.png)\n\nThis integral seems too challenging when written in Cartesian coordinates. However, we should recognize that the problem lends itself to spherical polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $R$ has a simple spherical polar representation, and\n\n- the integrand $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\\right)^{3/2}$ can also be expressed simply in spherical polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to spherical polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined by\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\n\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \n\nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2ed8a57c5e66687428a524eb148f96672f3a1ace7fbd28d426de065b2fb5f1f.png)\n\nTo express our integral in terms of spherical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x,y,z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant for a spherical change of variables is\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x,y,z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2\\sin\\phi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change of variables formula is\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\:  \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nNow, let's write our function $f(x,y,z) = \\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2\\right)^{3/2}$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{\\rho }^{6}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}(1-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b86207dc-5c0f-5e73-a894-7cd428b18036" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0355ca44-2058-5e43-b562-1ba67c747a89" :step/math-academy-id 42751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128841 :question/id #uuid "0e34fc48-0bfc-57b3-82fd-e5ed205995a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11372"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7a75441-d233-5a64-8d87-b3138c2d31be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049639}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496299}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "187ae894-50e1-5dc4-a7bd-e52db3a63568" :step/math-academy-id 42752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130228}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129265 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128842 :question/id #uuid "c56b3218-2c96-59fe-a5e3-af6f01164af4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11373"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1d5b6a-3e7b-567d-a61f-d18f55924912" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496318}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "6ef297ec-41d5-5d03-8b56-8189aecc9f8e" :step/math-academy-id 42753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130206}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129266 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128843 :question/id #uuid "85182b2a-10d4-5663-831b-41623c395cce" :question/math-academy-id "e-11374"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ea201b-cf90-54cf-bd20-d7e990345710" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496308}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Spherical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "6eb5bbde-3ae3-519e-a13a-011708f318d7" :step/math-academy-id 42755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130228 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128859 :question/id #uuid "2adb62b7-c889-5c4c-be0f-8f26a240475c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12409"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d85b11c-498d-53b7-98f8-b742b46e8dc2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496227}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Part-Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "fb036686-92a4-5d96-ae09-318717eb493f" :step/math-academy-id 44584 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130468 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've used the fact that \n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2 \\sin{\\phi}.\n$$\n\nLet's now prove this result.\n\nGiven the spherical coordinate transformations\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\n\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \n\nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi,\n$$\n\nwe first  calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta } & =-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂ϕ} & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂ϕ} & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂ϕ} & =-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & 0 & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ|\\begin{aligned}-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}|-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot (-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ))-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2 \\sin{\\phi}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86bd6cae-b4e6-5da9-80b3-b884c320bda3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7720 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Jacobian for Spherical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "96fb4483-0ca7-52c1-9f83-46c894da5471" :step/math-academy-id 44767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130469 :tutorial/content "We can also derive the change of variables formula for spherical polar coordinates using geometry.\n\nConsider a bounded spherical box $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\: | \\: a \\leq \\rho \\leq b, \\:\\: \\alpha \\leq \\theta \\leq \\beta, \\:\\: \\gamma \\leq \\phi \\leq \\psi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can divide each interval into $l,m,$ and $n$ subdivisions such that \n\n$$\n\\Delta\\rho = \\dfrac{b-a}{l},\\qquad \\Delta\\theta = \\dfrac{\\beta-\\alpha}{m},\\qquad \\Delta\\phi = \\dfrac{\\psi-\\gamma}{n}.\n$$\n\nThis splits the region into \"spherical wedges,\" as shown in the following diagram.\n\n![](images/88/88be4c6a09062321d20ac7f48dfbb6be796cdd6b5bb1b7f6adc49a6ae1da609b.png)\n\nZooming in on one of these wedges, we obtain a shape resembling a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/5e/5e5f7acb4d057e856c66e9d5626f81178ca26c0b7ce83cdf7290b1f4c64b1852.png)\n\nLet's note the volume of this solid as $\\Delta V.$ To approximate the value of $\\Delta V,$ we use the formula for the volume of a rectangular solid:\n\n$$\n\\Delta V \\approx lwh\n$$\n\nClearly, the height of the rectangular solid is $h = \\Delta \\rho.$ We can find the remaining dimensions using simple geometry. \n\nFirst, we need to find the length of the radius $r$ in the $xy$-plane. Using the following triangle and a side-on view of the wedge, we conclude that $r=\\rho\\sin\\phi.$\n\n![](images/01/0122c6c3b813bbe2821dbede794b6027cd69002dd1828925811211045a0d67e8.png)\n\nLet's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/d4/d41b538e9bb3add9bae1e3a24c9f87ae282a8b969f0c0dc4acd374ad777de74a.png)\n\nFinally, we can use the arc length formula $s = r \\cdot \\theta$ to find the length $l$ and width $w.$\n\n![](images/88/882b9243d1189c098aaf6e1e45ca7ec97d480b1f2c3a8fe23d5e39a6c2313ed2.png)\n\nThe length of our wedge is \n\n$$\nl = \\rho \\cdot \\Delta\\phi = \\rho\\Delta\\phi\n$$\n\n and the width is \n\n$$\nw = \\rho\\sin\\phi \\cdot \\Delta\\theta = \\rho\\sin\\phi\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nCalculating the volume as a rectangular solid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ΔV & ≈lwh \\\\  & =(\\rho Δϕ)\\cdot (\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕΔ\\theta )\\cdot (Δ\\rho ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,Δϕ\\,Δ\\theta \\,Δ\\rho .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we define the triple integral in spherical coordinates as the limit of a triple Riemann sum, where $(\\rho_{ijk},\\theta_{ijk},\\phi_{ijk})$ being any sample point in the spherical subregion $B{ijk}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{l,m,n \\to \\infty} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{l} \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^{m} \\sum\\limits_{k=1}^{n} f(\\rho_{ijk},\\theta_{ijk},\\phi_{ijk}) (\\rho_{ijk})^2 \\sin \\phi_{ijk} \\:\\Delta\\rho\\:\\Delta\\theta\\:\\Delta\\phi\n$$\n\nAs we increase the number of subregions, the size of $\\Delta\\rho,$ $\\Delta\\theta,$ and $\\Delta\\phi$ all goes to zero, and the volume of $R$ is more accurately approximated, giving\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}V = \\rho^2 \\sin\\phi \\:\\textrm{d}\\rho\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\\:\\textrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that if $f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ is continuous on a spherical box $R = [a,b] \\times [\\alpha,\\beta] \\times [\\gamma, \\psi],$ then\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\rho^2 \\sin\\phi \\:\\textrm{d}\\rho\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\\:\\textrm{d}\\phi\n=\n\\int_{\\gamma}^{\\psi}\\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta}\\int_{a}^{b} f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\rho^2 \\sin\\phi \\:\\textrm{d}\\rho\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\\:\\textrm{d}\\phi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "444d88e7-62f3-5333-98ad-9ee9e22d6a46" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7730 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Change of Variables Formula Using Geometry"} :step/id #uuid "68b6f2fa-a8c8-5b52-98b8-fd02b1454611" :step/math-academy-id 44789}] :activity/title "Triple Integrals in Spherical Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129390}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496371} :activity/id #uuid "f3fc645b-e16d-5d6f-90ed-54d6d3b27ea8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050390 :topic/id #uuid "4543eb7d-4646-5b7f-9bb9-14d0a8526a45" :topic/math-academy-id 2060} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0782d6f6-9606-57ec-9b7b-5f331ab47d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495529} {:answer/id #uuid "844ba137-ce42-54c2-ad73-0a6795c8a9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495794} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e1bc82-3dca-5d12-a26c-e853c58acc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi }{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495972} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f9f8b0-bd2d-567a-a334-e4d0096c528c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495983} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f5beda-e35c-5b36-a994-c61647e4e3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi }{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495794} :answer-field/id #uuid "452ba3e6-053b-531a-8d38-cad4a5db1e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2868a77-a9f6-5df5-bf81-a733f20b3a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161765" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "63749430-c9f6-5d26-a7e1-481aa4f8c432" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021f15c9-f5ca-5fc3-8d40-1a3115937ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495513} {:answer/id #uuid "02d47acc-5e12-5a9c-bd05-2023d477bc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495517} {:answer/id #uuid "18832182-39dc-5364-9464-ffd9aaaa5c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495557} {:answer/id #uuid "821f4bda-e9cb-5812-adda-f108cac57aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495789} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e840a3-e6d2-5b8d-b91c-2840c23ac88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495513} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f2e7f56-ee2d-5dfd-af32-c2c72dce6d2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4c2ec08-50ad-518f-bd3d-48075268a2f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161771" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2fd023af7c229d813344ee366b384a5917b69df0d06fabff6466b9ebcc199bb9.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\le 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/09/090f210768ffd4a6be529860b54a2906e265427b7d85bcbb07e3101c801740ba.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=\\pi /2}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =\\pi }^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{7}{\\rho }^{7}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2} \\\\  & =-(-1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}(128-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{128}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b10d38c-406c-548c-b35d-cf1e229f05a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a717243-48be-536d-910a-6d83ab68f329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495536} {:answer/id #uuid "1afd572b-1bde-5282-aebe-538e26120c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495564} {:answer/id #uuid "5a35fc1c-68fd-5d54-b891-f97bb27891a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495714} {:answer/id #uuid "90820d05-7799-52ea-bb46-3e07581e83ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495829} {:answer/id #uuid "cf09a2be-f3f1-58fe-aff8-83664e28906f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495829} :answer-field/id #uuid "851dbeb9-5199-5c05-92dd-f09fe944b1b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07d5c9c5-374d-5c01-b3ec-d01486174f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-161744" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/d2/d2b9c6f3541c47f37ad7f2ba52d739787e39600ec71fe3530f0ad2846c24880b.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a5f82725-c8bc-52c2-b33d-594e53b847fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b76607-eca3-5a84-8112-1e82b79c1646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495561} {:answer/id #uuid "3454506d-b82b-5713-b11f-01ee75904e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495633} {:answer/id #uuid "37432da4-1104-5db3-8f3f-d145e4ff681b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495638} {:answer/id #uuid "c1695142-7e5b-54c7-a0c8-e74c43072eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495936} {:answer/id #uuid "c737b74c-b7cd-528f-8697-a21b09e1b183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495946} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb52fe98-2353-5c03-b8bc-f23854e8e611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b06611cf-54db-58f0-8277-482f7aa27724" :question/math-academy-id "q-161753" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0845655-b076-5af2-8d0f-f85412655856" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c3d150-55f6-525d-b55a-6951b89555eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495516} {:answer/id #uuid "0641edc9-9f46-539c-b517-a7dff5678e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495527} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7b57ca-d67c-5580-9698-a81cf1ce11e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495813} {:answer/id #uuid "9a52e985-8c43-591c-a2b1-487e13e3bbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495854} {:answer/id #uuid "b2362d0a-41f0-5512-8b16-672410d72346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495527} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbd266d-a5fa-5b3b-84b2-668b33154b73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3dbf795-87d9-5ee7-946c-df26586b5ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161860" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d2a71ffdce7f02cdfa78881c7e355c9aada4134cf06b663a2deefb3d779b9ca.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8x,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/04/0475e2e272a30318dc4cb31457539a312544b6e14d7e6a8ed3f911d41404b575.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b765debc-aaac-5aa1-af3c-f378ddb7464a"}] :activity/title "Triple Integrals in Spherical Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495508}]}
